Subject: [PATCH v11 1/8] jails/instances/lab-mail: Initial spec
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Change-Id: rlrysuruvryxyvntzzsmyzxntuyxxkuv

Agents need to send and read p7x.dev mail, but the Fastmail
credentials should not live in any jail an agent works in. A
standalone spec settles what the relay jail does before anything is
built, so the design can be reviewed and changed in one place.

The agent must learn when a send fails and why. A queuing MTA
answers 250 before it talks to Fastmail, so a later rejection only
comes back as a bounce the agent may never see. smtprelay relays
during the client's session instead, and passes Fastmail's reply
through. smtprelay also checks the sender and recipients against
allowlists before it contacts Fastmail, so mistakes fail fast with a
clear reply. The cost is that nothing holds mail while Fastmail is
unreachable; the client gets a 4xx reply and must send again later.

Reading goes through an append-only local copy of the lists folder.
Clients can never change Fastmail, reading keeps working while
Fastmail is down, and deleting mail on Fastmail loses nothing.
Clients use IMAP rather than a shared mount, so agents need not run
on beastie.

The spec also lists the alternatives each decision rejects, and why,
so a reader can tell what lab-mail does not do and need not reopen a
settled choice.
